ach other on the circuit  you know and it reminds me of that time  when  there was a bit of a controversy because  the the u.s ladies professional golf  association said that that the korean  girls who were doing very well winning  lots of tournaments should really try  harder to speak english so that when  they're interviewed or when they play in  these pro-am tournaments that they can  speak english and so there was the usual  oh this is racist this is unfair this  isn't so i'm saying what are you talking  about they make their living  playing golf  uh people sponsor  uh  yeah there might be tv action in korea  but there's a huge market in north  america learn english it's that simple  and you know what today most those girls  speak very good english whether they're  korean thai there's so many good asian  golfers on the uh ladies golf tournament  circuit chinese you name it and they all  speak english very very well  so i don't think athletes have a special  talent for language learning i think  it's a matter of attitude mood we're  just going to do it it's no big deal  and  they managed to speak  so i had a few other things but i'm  going to save them for  another occasion oh the other one thing  i wanted to say was you know language  learning is about emotion it's about  context and now that i'm back at our  place here in palm springs  somehow i think i should be learning  polish because my sort of emotional  connection was when i was last here i  was going through my polish challenge  and i was riding on my you know my bike  and i was eating even hitting golf balls  at the range listening to polish somehow  this is my polish space but now it's not  it's my korean space so there you have  it thanks for listening bye for now

